-- title:
--   Quotient by a finite subgroup: dual pair and kernel ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of prime characteristic $q'$, algebraic over $\mathbb{Z}/q'$, and let $X_0$ and $W$ be Weierstrass curves over $\kappa$ that are elliptic. Assume every point of $W$ over $\kappa$ killed by $q'$ is zero, let $\chi$ be an arbitrary additive map from the points of $X_0$ to the points of $W$ (both after base change to $\kappa$), and let $C$ be a subgroup of the points of $W$ whose underlying set is finite. Here a homomorphism of point groups lies in `rationalHomSet` when it is zero or is given, off a finite set of $x$-coordinates, by fixed bivariate rational expressions in the coordinates, and `rationalEndSubring` is the subring of the additive endomorphisms of the points of $X_0$ generated by such self-maps. The assertion is that there exist an elliptic Weierstrass curve $V$ over $\kappa$ and additive maps $\varphi$ from the points of $W$ to those of $V$ and $\varphi'$ back, both in the respective `rationalHomSet`, such that: $\ker\varphi = C$; every rational $\alpha$ from $W$ to an elliptic $W_3$ vanishing on $C$ factors as $\varphi$ followed by some rational $\beta$ from $V$ to $W_3$; $\varphi$ followed by $\varphi'$ and $\varphi'$ followed by $\varphi$ are both multiplication by $\operatorname{card} C$; and the set of elements of `rationalEndSubring` of $X_0$ of the form $\chi$ followed by $\varphi$ followed by some rational $\rho\colon V \to X_0$ coincides with the set of those of the form $\chi$ followed by some rational $\sigma\colon W \to X_0$ vanishing on $C$.
--
--   This is the construction of the quotient elliptic curve $V = W/C$ by a finite subgroup, together with the dual isogeny in the weak form $\varphi'\varphi = \varphi\varphi' = |C|$, and the identification of the kernel ideal attached to the composite $\varphi\chi$ as the set of composites $\sigma\chi$ with $\sigma$ killing $C$. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd part of the development, where kernel ideals are realised by isogenies with cyclic kernel.
